# 2 Data Link Layer 

  The Data Link Layer is the 2nd layer in the OSI model, sitting between the Physical Layer and the Network Layer.

  It is responsible for node-to-node delivery, meaning it sends data from one directly connected device to another without errors.

  It converts raw bits from the Physical Layer into structured frames and ensures they are delivered in the right order without corruption.


📌 Two Sublayers
LLC (Logical Link Control)

Identifies the protocol being used (IP, ARP, etc.).
Controls frame synchronization and flow.

MAC (Media Access Control)

Controls how devices access the shared medium.
Uses MAC addresses for identification.
Example: Ethernet MAC.


📌 Main Responsibilities
Framing – Breaks bits into frames with header/trailer.

Physical Addressing – Adds MAC address to identify sender and receiver.

Error Detection & Correction – Uses Parity, CRC, Hamming.

Flow Control – Prevents data overflow.

Access Control – Decides which device can transmit when multiple share the same link.

Reliable Delivery – Ensures frames are delivered without corruption.


📌 Key Topics in Detail
1. Switching Techniques
Circuit Switching – Dedicated path for entire communication (telephone systems).

Packet Switching – Data split into packets, each routed independently (internet).

Message Switching – Entire message sent and stored at each hop before forwarding (store-and-forward).

2. VLAN (Virtual LAN)
Creates separate logical networks inside a single physical network.

Improves security and reduces congestion.

3. Link Aggregation
Combines multiple physical connections into one logical link.
Benefits: Higher speed, redundancy.

4. Framing
Data is packaged into frames with:
Header → Source & destination MAC, control info.
Payload → Actual data.
Trailer → Error-checking bits (CRC).

5. Error Detection/Correction
Parity Bit – Detects single-bit errors.
CRC – Detects burst errors.
Hamming Code – Detects & corrects errors.

6. Flow Control
Stop-and-Wait – Send 1 frame at a time, wait for ACK.
Sliding Window – Send multiple frames before waiting.
Go-Back-N – Resend from error frame onwards.
Selective Repeat – Resend only erroneous frame.

7. Piggybacking
ACK is sent along with outgoing data instead of separately.


📌 Protocols in Data Link Layer
Ethernet
PPP (Point-to-Point Protocol)
HDLC (High-Level Data Link Control)
Frame Relay
ATM (Asynchronous Transfer Mode)


📌 Advantages
Reliable node-to-node delivery.
Detects and corrects transmission errors.
Controls congestion with flow control.
Organizes data for easier transmission.


📌 Disadvantages
Adds extra overhead (headers, trailers).
Limited to directly connected devices.
Cannot handle routing (done in Network Layer).


📌 Real-Life Examples
Switches → Operate at Data Link Layer.

NIC (Network Interface Card) → Uses MAC address for communication.

